  {
    "id": "faraday_q005",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series I §34",
    "question": "What was the direction of the induced current when the primary circuit was made, as Faraday stated in §34? Quote his exact phrase, and what modern law do these observations prefigure?",
    "gold_answer": "On making the primary circuit, the induced current flowed 'in the opposite direction to that which the primary combination would have produced.' On breaking the circuit the induced current was in the same direction as the primary. These observations prefigure Lenz's Law (1834): the induced current always opposes the change that produces it.",
    "expected_points": [
      "exact phrase: 'in the opposite direction to that which the primary combination would have produced'",
      "on making: opposite direction; on breaking: same direction",
      "prefigures Lenz's Law (1834)"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q006",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series I §55–71",
    "question": "Describe the first electromagnetic generator Faraday built in §55–71: what were its dimensions, what was novel about its output, and how did he verify it?",
    "gold_answer": "Faraday built a copper disc 12 inches in diameter, rotated between the poles of a horseshoe magnet, with electrical contacts at the rim and at the axle. Unlike all previous induction effects (which were transient), this produced a continuous (non-transient) EMF. He verified it with a galvanometer deflection and a chemical decomposition test; the current was proportional to rotational speed.",
    "expected_points": [
      "copper disc 12 inches in diameter",
      "rotated between poles of horseshoe magnet",
      "contacts at rim and at axle",
      "produced continuous non-transient EMF — first of its kind",
      "verified by galvanometer and chemical decomposition",
      "current proportional to rotational speed"
    ],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q007",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series II §113",
    "question": "What were the dimensions of the bar magnets Faraday used to repeat all his magneto-electric experiments in §113, and why does he mention them?",
    "gold_answer": "The bar magnets were two feet long, one inch and a half wide, and half an inch thick. Faraday mentions them to show that the large Royal Society magnet was not essential — all the revolving-plate and induction experiments could be repeated 'with the most striking results' using these ordinary bar magnets.",
