    "id": "faraday_q008",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series II §188–189",
    "question": "Describe Faraday's Waterloo Bridge experiment in §188–189: what was the length of wire used, what did he hope to detect, and what was the outcome?",
    "gold_answer": "Faraday extended 'a copper wire nine hundred and sixty feet in length upon the parapet of the bridge', connecting its ends to plates in the Thames. He hoped to detect electric currents induced by the tidal flow of water cutting the Earth's magnetic field lines. After three days of experiments he obtained only irregular deflections attributable to temperature differences, water purity variations, and contact effects — not the sought tidal currents.",
    "expected_points": [
      "copper wire 960 feet long on parapet of Waterloo Bridge",
      "plates dipped into Thames water",
      "hoped to detect tidal induced currents",
      "result: irregular deflections only, inconclusive after three days"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q009",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series III §371",
    "question": "State the exact dimensions Faraday used in §371 for his platina-zinc wire experiment, the composition of the acid, and how much electricity was produced.",
    "gold_answer": "The platina and zinc wires were each one-eighteenth of an inch in diameter, placed five-sixteenths of an inch apart, immersed to five-eighths of an inch depth in acid consisting of one drop of oil of vitriol and four ounces of distilled water at about 60°F, connected by a copper wire 18 feet long and one-eighteenth of an inch thick. This arrangement yielded electricity equal to a Leyden battery charged by thirty turns of a large plate electric machine.",
    "expected_points": [
      "1/18 inch diameter (both wires)",
      "5/16 inch apart",
      "5/8 inch depth of immersion",
      "one drop oil of vitriol in four ounces distilled water at 60°",
      "18-foot copper connector, 1/18 inch thick",
      "equivalent to Leyden battery charged by 30 turns of large machine"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q010",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series IV §381–395",
    "question": "What did Faraday discover about ice in Series IV §381, what battery did he use to test it, and how general did he find the law in §394–395?",
    "gold_answer": "Faraday found that ice is an electrical non-conductor: even a thin film of ice in the circuit of a very powerful voltaic battery stopped all current flow and chemical decomposition. He tested with a battery of up to 150 pairs of four-inch plates — still no current passed through the ice. In §394 he found the law was general: liquefaction confers conducting power, congelation removes it. In §395 he verified this for chloride of lead: fused, it conducted; solid, it did not.",
    "expected_points": [
      "ice is a non-conductor of electricity",
      "tested with up to 150 pairs of four-inch plates",
      "law of liquefaction: conducting power acquired on melting, lost on freezing",
      "§395: verified for fused chloride of lead"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q011",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series VII §861–862",
    "question": "What was Faraday's calculation in §861 about the quantity of electricity needed to decompose one grain of water, and what Leyden battery did he use as his reference?",
    "gold_answer": "Faraday calculated in §861 that 800,000 charges of his reference Leyden battery would be necessary to supply enough electricity to decompose a single grain of water. His reference Leyden battery was one charged by thirty turns of a very large and powerful plate electric machine in full action (as established in §371).",
    "expected_points": [
      "800,000 Leyden battery charges needed",
      "to decompose one grain of water",
      "Leyden battery reference: charged by 30 turns of large plate machine",
      "this electricity is naturally associated with the atoms in that water"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
