    "id": "faraday_q012",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series VII §864–868",
    "question": "In §864 Faraday weighed two amalgamated zinc plates for his electrochemical equivalent experiment. What were the exact weights and dimensions of the plates, and what electrochemical equivalent did he obtain in §868?",
    "gold_answer": "Plate A weighed 163.1 grains; plate B weighed 148.3 grains. Both were about five inches long and 0.4 of an inch wide. From the experiment Faraday determined in §868 that 'the chemical action upon 32.31 parts, or one equivalent of zinc' was attended by the evolution of the same quantity of electricity as 800,000 charges of the reference Leyden battery. The electrochemical equivalent of zinc is therefore 32.31 parts (grains).",
    "expected_points": [
      "plate A: 163.1 grains",
      "plate B: 148.3 grains",
      "both 5 inches long and 0.4 inch wide",
      "electrochemical equivalent of zinc = 32.31 parts"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q013",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series IX §1049–1064",
    "question": "What was the original observation by Mr. Jenkin that led Faraday to discover self-induction (§1049), and what dimensions were the plain copper wire he tested in §1064?",
    "gold_answer": "Jenkin observed that if a short ordinary wire connects a single pair of metals, no shock is felt on breaking the circuit; but if the wire of an electro-magnet is used instead, a strong shock is felt each time the contact is broken. In §1064 Faraday tested self-induction in a plain (non-coiled) wire: 'A copper wire, 0.18 of an inch in diameter, and 132 feet in length, was laid out upon the floor of the laboratory.' On breaking contact it produced a bright spark, and with silver plates at the ends could convulse a frog.",
    "expected_points": [
      "Jenkin: shock from breaking electro-magnet circuit, not from plain wire",
      "§1064: copper wire 0.18 inch diameter",
      "132 feet in length, laid on laboratory floor",
      "produced spark on breaking, could convulse a frog"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q014",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series XI §1218–1224",
    "question": "Describe the shell-lac cylinder experiment Faraday performed in §1218 to prove induction in curved lines: what were the dimensions of the cylinder and what did the measurements at positions k, l, m show?",
    "gold_answer": "Faraday used 'a cylinder of solid shell-lac, 0.9 of an inch in diameter and seven inches in length, fixed upright in a wooden foot', with the upper half excited negatively by friction with warm flannel. Using a carrier ball at positions k (108°), l (65°), m (35°) around the brass hemisphere placed on top (§1224), he showed that inductive force existed at positions not directly above the excited surface, proving that lines of inductive force follow curved paths — not straight lines.",
    "expected_points": [
      "shell-lac cylinder 0.9 inch diameter and 7 inches long",
      "excited negatively by friction with warm flannel",
      "carrier ball measurements at k=108°, l=65°, m=35°",
      "proved induction follows curved lines, not straight"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "id": "faraday_q015",
    "category": "specific_fact",
    "source": "Faraday Series XI §1256–1260",
    "question": "What numerical value did Faraday calculate for the specific inductive capacity of shell-lac in §1260, using what apparatus, and why was the result 'so unexpected in its amount'?",
    "gold_answer": "Using two inductive hemisphere apparatus (app. i. with shell-lac, app. ii. with air), Faraday divided a charge and found the shell-lac apparatus received 176 degrees while the air apparatus received 113 degrees. The SIC of shell-lac = 176/113 = 1.558 relative to air. The result was unexpected because previous electrical theory had assumed all insulators were equivalent; it proved for the first time that different dielectrics have intrinsically different capacities for induction.",
    "expected_points": [
      "shell-lac SIC = 176/113 = approximately 1.558 relative to air",
      "apparatus: two inductive hemisphere apparatus (one with shell-lac, one with air)",
      "176 degrees vs 113 degrees after division",
      "unexpected because previous theory assumed all insulators equivalent"
    ],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  }
]
